<Base title='How our field notes work'><div class="container"><div class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">The publication</p><h1>How our field notes work</h1></div><article class="prose section"><p>We prioritize official campground operators, Wisconsin DNR and reservation systems for changing facts. A source ledger records what a source supports and when it was accessed. Reader discussions can suggest questions but do not establish current policies.</p><h2>Facts, interpretation and unknowns</h2><p>Profile recommendations explain the practical implications of published information. They are identified as editorial planning judgments, not firsthand observations. Unknown information stays unknown; an unverified pool is not marked absent, and Wi-Fi availability would not establish reliable speed.</p><p>Accessible facilities are described according to published information. Readers should discuss their own needs with the operator. A cabin with shared bathrooms is different from a cabin with a bathroom inside.</p><h2>Dates and corrections</h2><p>Created, published, substantially modified and facts-reviewed dates are separate fields. A redeployment does not refresh a review date. Volatile facts enter a review-due report; automated source differences should trigger a human review, not silently rewrite recommendations.</p><p>Contact us with the campground name, the fact to check and a reliable source. Reports are reviewed before editorial changes are made. We do not run an unrestricted reader-review platform.</p><h2>Independence and images</h2><p>Booking commissions do not determine whether a public campground is covered. We label real paid links and sponsorships near recommendations. Initial park photos are openly licensed and credited; they do not imply that we visited the location or that the photo shows a particular site. Original reporting and owner-supplied photographs are future additions.</p></article></div></Base>
